  "comments": [
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/cloud/samlo/couch/kevbox/restart/ForceStop.kt",
      "content": "`WindowRoot.root: Any` and the `Any?` return value drop the type, so `root()` has to cast back with `as AccessibilityNodeInfo?`. If anyone builds a `WindowRoot` with a different root type, that cast fails at runtime. Making the class and the function generic keeps the helper testable on the JVM and removes the cast.",
      "suggestion_code": "internal data class WindowRoot\u003cT : Any\u003e(val pkg: String?, val isApplication: Boolean, val root: T)\n\ninternal fun \u003cT : Any\u003e pickSettingsRoot(windows: List\u003cWindowRoot\u003cT\u003e\u003e, active: WindowRoot\u003cT\u003e?, settings: Set\u003cString\u003e): T? =",
      "existing_code": "internal fun pickSettingsRoot(windows: List\u003cWindowRoot\u003e, active: WindowRoot?, settings: Set\u003cString\u003e): Any? =",
      "start_line": 26,
      "end_line": 26,
      "category": "maintainability",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/cloud/samlo/couch/kevbox/restart/ForceStop.kt",
      "content": "Every 100 ms poll fetches `window.root` for every window on screen, including the launcher, the system UI and the IME. Each fetch is a separate binder call. It also always calls `rootInActiveWindow`, even when a Settings window has already been found. On a low-end TV box that is a lot of IPC per tick. Make the window list lazy (`asSequence()`), and only read the active window when no Settings window turned up.",
      "existing_code": "        val windows = service.windows.mapNotNull { window -\u003e\n            window.root?.let { WindowRoot(it.packageName?.toString(), window.type == AccessibilityWindowInfo.TYPE_APPLICATION, it) }\n        }\n        val active = service.rootInActiveWindow?.let { WindowRoot(it.packageName?.toString(), true, it) }",
      "start_line": 123,
      "end_line": 126,
      "category": "performance",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/cloud/samlo/couch/HomeAccessibilityService.kt",
      "content": "`VolumeRepeat` sees the key before `KeyFilter` decides whether to consume it. `KeyMap.parse` accepts any positive keycode, so a provisioned entry can bind 24 or 25. In that case the filter consumes the press to open an app, but holding the key still starts a ramp that changes the volume. Only feed the ramp when the filter did not consume the event.",
      "suggestion_code": "        val consumed = filter?.onKey(event.action, event.keyCode, event.scanCode, event.repeatCount) ?: false\n        if (!consumed) volume?.onKey(event.action, event.keyCode, event.repeatCount)\n        return consumed",
      "existing_code": "        volume?.onKey(event.action, event.keyCode, event.repeatCount)\n        return filter?.onKey(event.action, event.keyCode, event.scanCode, event.repeatCount) ?: false",
      "start_line": 37,
      "end_line": 38,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/cloud/samlo/couch/kevbox/keys/VolumeRepeat.kt",
      "content": "This relies on the platform no longer repeating filtered keys. The comment above says repeats \"the system still sends\" are ignored, but those repeats still pass on to the system: `filter.onKey` returns false for unmapped keys. On any device or firmware where they arrive, the system steps the volume on each repeat and `tick` steps it as well, so the ramp runs at double speed. A cheap safeguard: when a DOWN with `repeatCount \u003e 0` arrives for the held key, call `stop()`, because the system is already ramping.",
      "suggestion_code": "            DOWN -\u003e if (repeatCount == 0) {\n                cancel()\n                held = keyCode\n                schedule(timeoutMs, ::tick)\n            } else if (keyCode == held) {\n                // The platform is repeating on its own; don't double the ramp.\n                stop()\n            }",
      "existing_code": "            DOWN -\u003e if (repeatCount == 0) {\n                cancel()\n                held = keyCode\n                schedule(timeoutMs, ::tick)\n            }",
      "start_line": 28,
      "end_line": 32,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/cloud/samlo/couch/HomeAccessibilityService.kt",
      "content": "`onServiceConnected` replaces `volume` without stopping the old instance. If the service rebinds without `onDestroy` (unbind/rebind) while a ramp is running, the old instance keeps `held` set and `tick` keeps rescheduling itself. The volume would then run to max or min until some later volume DOWN calls `removeCallbacksAndMessages`. Call `volume?.stop()` before creating the new instance.",
      "suggestion_code": "        val audio = getSystemService(AudioManager::class.java)\n        volume?.stop()\n        volume = VolumeRepeat(",
      "existing_code": "        val audio = getSystemService(AudioManager::class.java)\n        volume = VolumeRepeat(",
      "start_line": 56,
      "end_line": 57,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/res/xml/home_accessibility.xml",
      "content": "The rest of this comment (\"a key with no mapping passes through untouched, so holding volume ramps\") is now wrong. This change exists because filtering keys stops Android repeating a held key, and the new class doc in `HomeAccessibilityService` says the ramp comes from `VolumeRepeat`. Please update the comment so nobody later deletes `VolumeRepeat` thinking the platform does the ramp.",
      "suggestion_code": "     hence flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows). Key events are filtered so the remote's app buttons\n     open KevBox's apps; a key with no mapping passes through, and VolumeRepeat makes the held-volume\n     ramp because filtering stops the platform's own key repeat. --\u003e",
      "existing_code": "     hence flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows). Key events are filtered so the remote's app buttons",
      "start_line": 4,
      "end_line": 4,
      "category": "documentation",
      "severity": "low"
    }
